/-!
# The unit-step results with one derivative size

## Declarations

- `evolution_over_time`
-/

@[expose] public section

open scoped BigOperators Matrix.Norms.L2Operator Topology
open Complex Set Real MeasureTheory Matrix Filter
noncomputable section
namespace UnitaryProducts.Paper
open UnitaryProducts UnitaryProducts.Dressed UnitaryProducts.Corrector
variable {d : ℕ}
local notation "𝕄" => Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℂ

/-- **The evolution of a path over time $T$** (§2, unnumbered). The evolution of a path $H$ over
time $T$ is the evolution $U$ generated by $H(t/T)$ on $[0,T]$. By the chain rule,
$s\mapsto U(Ts)$ is generated by $TH$ on $[0,1]$, so it and $U(T)$ depend only on $TH$.

Reading the statement against the paper:
* `hU` says that `U` is the evolution of $H$ over time $T$:
  `IsEvolution (fun t ↦ H (t / T)) U 0 T`, generated by $H(t/T)$ on $[0,T]$.
* The conclusion is the chain-rule sentence: $TH$ (`fun s ↦ T • H s`) generates
  $s\mapsto U(Ts)$ on $[0,1]$, whose value at $s=1$ is $U(T)$. That $s\mapsto U(Ts)$ and $U(T)$
  depend only on $TH$ then follows from the uniqueness of evolutions
  (`exists_unique_evolution`, for regulated $TH$), which is not restated here. The rescaling of
  §4.2, that $U(T)$ "is also the final value of the evolution of $(T/N)H$ over time $N$", is
  `rescaled_evolution_steps`.
* No regularity hypothesis appears: the change of variable needs none.

The proof is `rescaled_evolution_steps` with $N=1$: the time change $t\mapsto Ts$. -/
theorem evolution_over_time {H U : ℝ → 𝕄} {T : ℝ} (hT : 0 < T)
    (hU : IsEvolution (fun t ↦ H (t / T)) U 0 T) :
    IsEvolution (fun s ↦ T • H s) (fun s ↦ U (T * s)) 0 1 := by
  simpa only [Nat.cast_one, div_one] using (rescaled_evolution_steps hT 1 one_pos hU).1

end UnitaryProducts.Paper
